description:
memory.rs:512-517 — position handling:

    let start = if position >= 0 {
        (position as usize).min(ids.len())
    } else {
        let neg = position.saturating_neg() as usize;
        ids.len().saturating_sub(neg)
    };

Three smells in 5 lines:
1.  after the  guard — assumes i64 fits in usize on 32-bit targets (it doesn't, but the workspace MSRV/target is 64-bit so this is theoretical). The cast is lossy.
2.  — saturating_neg returns i64::MIN unchanged (it cannot represent +i64::MAX+1), and casting i64::MIN to usize gives a giant number that saturating_sub then floors to 0. Works by accident, hard to reason about.
3. The two-branch structure is just (max 0 ... len) clamping with an offset-from-end alternative. Could be a single expression using i64-to-len arithmetic.

Suggested rewrite (clearer, no as-casts):

    let start = if position >= 0 {
        usize::try_from(position).unwrap_or(usize::MAX).min(ids.len())
    } else {
        // -N means 'N from end', clamped at 0.
        let neg = position.unsigned_abs() as usize;
        ids.len().saturating_sub(neg)
    };

i64::unsigned_abs() returns u64 deterministically including for i64::MIN (returns 9223372036854775808). The conversion to usize is then the bounded one — and on 64-bit targets it's exact. The  path catches the 32-bit usize::MAX-overflow case explicitly.

Bonus: position is declared as i64 in the trait signature (memory.rs:491). RFC 8620 §5.5 specifies position as a signed integer with sign convention (negative = from end). The current impl is correct on the happy path; the smells are about reading-it-correctly cost. Strictly P3.

Same pattern is likely in canonical jmap-mail-server (bd:JMAP-x2gd.53 already covers as-casts in chat-server) — worth checking if this should be a canonical-template sweep across query_objects in all 8 server crates. If so, file as a workspace finding under JMAP-x2gd.67 (workspace canonical-template re-classification).
